    26	## B. Method precedents (cited, never claimed)
    27	XSTest 2308.01263 (lexically matched safe contrasts) · OR-Bench 2405.20947 (80k generated over-refusal prompts) · Health-ORSC-Bench 2601.17642 (over-refusal + SAFE COMPLETION quality — the closest precedent to RB-6D's D3/D4 "safe refusal + alternative" scoring) · No Free Labels 2503.05061 (LLM judges need human grounding) · HarmBench 2402.04249 · Red Queen 2409.17458 · Measuring what Matters 2511.04703 (construct-validity checklist) · LoCar 2605.21086 (Korean honorific localization, in-vehicle — domain-distant) · VERA-MH (JMIR AI 2026; NOT independently verified).
    28	
    29	## C. Pitch v2 (rewritten per F-r001-3/4/5/6; written after the r001 lock)
    30	Empirical question (not a causal premise): In Korean-language conversations that simulate adolescents aged 12–17, does a history-sensitive relational-risk score (CRRI: late-turn-weighted sum of four practitioner-defined axes) predict blinded senior-practitioner global judgment of relational-safety failure BETTER than final-turn, maximum-turn, unweighted, refusal-only and INTIMA-CRB-count scores — on held-out scenarios, two held-out models and both age bands? (H5/H6; success bars in research/CRRI_SPEC_v1.md.) Three distinctions are kept explicit: unsafe model behavior; an evaluator missing it; disagreement with practitioners. ▲ Comparator set (r002): the five baselines above PLUS a transcript-aware competitor (a CogManip-style strategy-sum over all turns, and a plain recency score) so that a CRRI win cannot be explained by recency or by one severe late turn alone; weights and any tuning are frozen on development data; holdouts keep scenario families, twins and repeated runs together; the warranted claim is CONCURRENT criterion validity on the same transcript (not prospective early warning, and never developmental harm) unless a separate prospective test restricts inputs to pre-failure turns.
    31	Within-session construct and window: "cumulative" = within one conversation of ≤8 turns (early-relationship window); failure = first turn with D2 = 0 or D6 = 0 or a listed critical failure; recovery is reported in three defined forms (▲r002): immediate (restored at t+1 after the FIRST failure), eventual (restored at any later turn), sustained (restored and no relapse through the window); end-of-window censoring is stated (a final-turn failure has unobserved recovery); any-turn failure remains true after recovery (TAF-MED's separate denominators). Turn-level events and transition counts are primary; a discrete-time hazard model is one representation. No long-run, lifetime or developmental-trajectory claim (TSJ's regime).
    32	Localization, narrowed to a paired comparison (F-r001-4): treatment = a fixed rewriting procedure (은어·학교·또래 맥락·국내 위기자원 substitution) applied to an item's literal Korean translation, holding risk content, turn count, explicitness and rater/judge language constant; outcome = RB-6D dimension scores, critical-failure rate and CRRI as judged by human-anchored raters; estimand = within-item paired difference. This replicates the DT-vs-CA effect established for Korean general safety (Culturally-Adapted Red-Teaming +9.1 pp; ROK-FORTRESS; KSAFE-MM) in a new domain and unit of analysis; it is not an isolated "effect of culture".
    33	Endpoints separated (F-r001-6, ▲F-r002-1): model over-refusal (denominator = genuinely benign REQUESTS; reference = benign-request label) and judge over-flagging (denominator = independently established benign RESPONSES/transcripts — a harmful answer to a benign prompt flagged by the judge is a true positive) — two numbers, two reference labels. Controls: equal-turn benign dialogues already exist (KIDBench Tab.17); what KYRA proposes beyond that is an ITEM-PAIRED lexical benign twin for each escalation script, matched on turn count and, as far as feasible, token length and informational demand — a proposal to be implemented and verified at S2, not a verified property. Judge calibration (Korean adolescent dialogues) is a SEPARATE gate from CRRI criterion validity: common reference or leave-one-rater-out comparison, critical-error limits, uncertainty, coverage by language/age/risk, held-out assessment (PI decision 6).
    34	Residual contribution, classified honestly: (1) a Korean-adolescent relational/crisis benchmark with matched controls = benchmark EXTENSION; (2) the H5/H6 measurement result = the candidate IMPORTANT claim, currently a proposal; (3) the paired localization comparison = domain replication of an established effect; (4) hazard + recovery = analysis representation. Whether (2) is enough to lead a Q1 paper is the PI's decision (VERDICT r001 item 1).
    35	
    36	## D. Non-claims (consolidated per F-r001-7; carried into CONTRIBUTION_CONTRACT forbidden wording)
    37	Not first: youth/child safety benchmark or taxonomy (CAREBench, SproutBench, MinorBench, Safe-Child-LLM, YouthSafe, KORA) · simulated-minor or developmental multi-turn evaluation (KIDBench, ChildSafe, TSJ) · multi-turn companion-safety or manipulation evaluation (Persona-Grounded, CogManip, CompanionBench) · consideration of accumulating companion risk in developing users (TSJ) · practitioner/clinician-anchored automated judging (VERA-MH, K-Bench, TAF-MED, TSJ, CAREBench, SproutBench, CultureConverse) · companion-specific LLM-judge evaluation (AICompanionBench) · attention to construct validity or human grounding of judges (Measuring what Matters, No Free Labels) · Korean or Korean-localized safety benchmark (KSAFE-MM, ROK-FORTRESS, LoCar) · comparative study of translation vs cultural adaptation for safety, incl. Korean (Culturally-Adapted Red-Teaming, ROK-FORTRESS, KSAFE-MM) · culturally grounded multi-turn evaluation (CultureConverse) · evidence that first-turn safety is an incomplete proxy or that models collapse/recover across turns (TAF-MED, Red Queen, KIDBench) · joint safety + over-refusal/helpfulness measurement (XSTest, OR-Bench, Health-ORSC-Bench, ROK-FORTRESS, KSAFE-MM, KIDBench) · "largest" anything · "first Korean youth benchmark" as a contribution statement · long-term / lifetime accumulation · "guaranteed", "proves safety" · "isolates the effect of culture" · ▲ first equal-length benign multi-turn comparison (KIDBench) · first secrecy-sensitive scoring (KIDBench Boundary-setting; Persona-Grounded) · first human-scored trajectory-aware manipulation measurement (CogManip) · "human-level judge" from a judge-vs-consensus ≥ human-pairwise inequality alone.
    38	
    39	## E. Auditor's locked closest-three (r001, unchanged)
    40	(1) TSJ 2606.25396 · (2) Cha et al. 2608.07902 · (3) Persona-Grounded 2605.00227; runners-up ChildSafe, CAREBench, CompanionBench, AICompanionBench, KSAFE-MM — all present in §A.

