    69	| F-r001-7 — incomplete non-claims | MINOR | **RESOLVED** | M:35–36 consolidate the requested exclusions, including multi-turn companion/manipulation work, accumulating developmental risks, construct validity, judge grounding, comparative localization, joint refusal/safety, “largest,” and “first Korean youth benchmark.” R:19 points to that list. Propagation into an unseen contribution-contract file is not independently verified. |
    70	
    71	**New findings**
    72	
    73	**F-r002-1 — MAJOR — The residual control gap is too broad, and secrecy overlap is understated.**
    74	
    75	Evidence: E:28 and R:10(c) say turn-matched benign controls were not found in the full texts. KIDBench explicitly runs five-turn dialogues (§6, p. 7, T:548–555), including the Benign category analyzed across turns 1–5 in Table 17 (p. 26, T:2066–2081). Equal-duration benign comparisons therefore precede KYRA. This does **not** establish that KIDBench constructs the proposed lexical, item-paired benign counterpart of each escalation script. The correct remaining distinction must identify that stronger pairing, rather than reserving all turn-matched benign evaluation.
    76	
    77	E:27 also limits scored secrecy content to Persona-Grounded's theme, although E:14 already notes KIDBench's boundary-setting criterion. The actual KIDBench rubric directly scores secrecy discouragement and penalizes secrecy encouragement (pp. 28, 31, T:2166–2174 and T:2336–2337). Distinguish a dedicated named axis from content already scored inside a broader axis.
    78	
    79	Required correction: acknowledge KIDBench's same-length benign trajectory comparison and secrecy-sensitive rubric; narrow any remaining absence claim to the exact proposed counterpart construction and validation. This reduces the novelty of components, without showing the full H5/H6 result already exists. Status: **OPEN**.
    80	
    81	**F-r002-2 — MAJOR — Human-validation comparisons are misattributed or treated as interchangeable.**
    82	
    83	Evidence: E:10/M:11 assign κ = 0.874 and 94.0% to automated-panel agreement with humans; R:8 repeats the κ in a judge–human list. ROK-FORTRESS §5.1.2 attributes these to two human annotators and reports κ = 0.736/F1 = 0.831 for the best overall panel against consensus (p. 9, T:454–473). App. E.7's r ≥ 0.836 is a different, pooled TRS endpoint and does not repair the wrong κ attribution.
    84	
    85	Related design consequence: M:16 proposes adopting “judge–human ≥ human–human” as acceptance logic. K-Bench's 94.2% compares a judge to adjudicated consensus, while 89.4% compares individual clinicians with each other, with different weighting/denominators (Table 1 note, p. 7). CultureConverse has the analogous reference difference and additionally uses ±1 agreement (Table 3). These remain useful reported calibration results, but an inequality across different comparisons is insufficient evidence of human equivalence. Nor does calibration of rubric scores establish independent validity of CRRI against a separate global criterion.
    86	
    87	Required correction: fix the ROK attribution; preserve the metric, reference, unit, denominator, language stratum, and calibration/test split for every statistic. The PI should approve a KYRA judge-calibration gate separately from the CRRI criterion-validity gate. A common independent reference, or a specified leave-one-rater-out comparison, is one defensible way to make a human baseline interpretable; critical-error rates and uncertainty still need explicit acceptance criteria. Status: **OPEN**.
    88	
    89	**F-r002-3 — MAJOR — CogManip needs a measurement comparison, not only a taxonomy citation.**
    90	
    91	Evidence: M:23 describes expert-validated scenarios and automated metrics, with language ND, and classifies the paper as a taxonomy reference. Its §3.2.2 includes human scoring of generated dialogues and an MRI outcome defined from changing user independence; App. B.3 reports AI/human total-score correlation 0.459 on 1,680 samples after standardization. §4.2.4 analyzes within-four-turn strategy timing, including later dependency/emotional-blackmail behavior. Table 3 and the appendix provide English/Chinese materials. These are relevant to the proposed short-session measurement result, beyond manipulation taxonomy.
    92	
    93	The distinction remains substantive but narrower: inspected CogManip passages use the same strategy framework for human and automated annotation and a simulated-user trajectory outcome. They do not establish independent senior-practitioner global relational-safety judgment plus held-out incremental validity against the proposed baselines. Its judge also sees assistant `thought` and `speak`, whereas an applicable KYRA comparison must state what observable information is available. The standardized correlation is not exact agreement or proof of MRI's external validity.
    94	
    95	Required correction: reconcile these methods explicitly in the matrix and decide whether a comparable full-transcript score is an H5/H6 baseline or justify its exclusion. A win over final-turn, maximum, unweighted, refusal-only, and CRB-count baselines alone would not establish superiority over existing transcript-aware measurement. This does not change the locked closest-three selection. Status: **OPEN**.
    96	
    97	**F-r002-4 — MINOR — Remaining evidence precision corrections.**
    98	
    99	| Evidence line | Correction |
   100	|---|---|
   101	| E:19; M:21 | Replace AICompanionBench's asserted adult-user scope with “user ages not established/no age-stratified sample reported in the inspected collection methods.” The annotator's age is not the users' age. Do not infer demonstrated minor coverage either. Flag 466 versus the PDF narrative's 465 safe cases as an unresolved source/record count discrepancy. |
   102	| M:12; R:24 | Separate KSAFE Fig. 2a's 0.22 pp contextualization increment from Table 4's 2.7 pp result. Translation quality is a plausible design concern, not identified by the English/Korean contrast; specify which experiment supports any “little contextualization effect” statement. |
   103	| E:25; R:8 | The eight-paper list is non-exhaustive: E:8/M:9 and Persona-Grounded §6.1 also report judge/human classifier validation, making at least nine among the original sixteen. Use “at least eight” for the listed examples or include the ninth. |
   104	| E:17; M:19 | Retain “no reported turn-index onset/persistence/recovery analysis” for ChildSafe; do not turn it into an absence of response-level scoring, which §3.3/Algorithm 1 explicitly supplies. |
   105	
   106	These corrections do not change the recommendation or establish an exact predecessor. Status: **OPEN; editorial corrections required**.
   107	
   108	**Adversarial assessment of Pitch v2**
   109	
   110	The empirical question in M:29 is well-posed as an S1 hypothesis: compare out-of-sample agreement/prediction of an independent practitioner criterion using a history-sensitive score and stated alternatives. The “fail because” overclaim is gone. However, the audit package does not contain the score formula/weights/normalization, full axis anchors, criterion decision rule, primary performance metric, improvement threshold, or definitions of the two age bands. M:29 refers to `research/CRRI_SPEC_v1.md`, which is outside the allowed input set. Those details are **not reviewed here**, rather than assumed absent from the project. No completed experiment is required to close S1, but the PI must approve a reviewable success contract.
   111	
   112	A weighted sum that beats final-turn and unweighted scores can demonstrate useful incremental criterion information. It does not by itself identify accumulation as the reason: weighting may capture recency, one severe late response, conversation length, or rater weighting preferences. Freeze weighting and all tuning on development data; keep scenario families, translated/localized twins, and repeated model runs together when constructing holdouts. State whether held-out scenarios and models form a joint holdout, and limit model-generalization claims to what the two selected held-out models support. Include an appropriate recency/full-transcript competitor or constrain the interpretation to the baselines actually tested. CogManip makes this choice concrete (F-r002-3).
   113	
   114	Blinded separate criterion raters (R:23) reduce direct score-copying and model halo. They do not make two instruments conceptually independent if the global label is mechanically defined from the same axis failures. Specify the global criterion in its own practitioner terms and report rater reliability. A full-transcript score predicting a global judgment of that same transcript is held-out criterion validation, not prospective prediction of future harm. If an evolving score is used to predict future failure, restrict its inputs to information available before that failure; later turns would leak the outcome. Agreement with practitioners is not observed adolescent harm.
   115	
   116	The within-session window is now explicit (M:30): ≤8 turns and no long-run/developmental trajectory claim. The definition is still only partly operational within this package: “turn,” D2, D6, the critical-failure list, and “boundary restored” need accessible anchors. A first-failure time is distinct from a recoverable safety state. In a SAFE→FAIL→SAFE conversation, any-turn failure remains true while next-turn restoration is observed; in SAFE→SAFE→FAIL at the final turn, restoration is unobserved. FAIL→FAIL→SAFE has eventual restoration but no immediate restoration after the first failure. The PI must choose whether recovery means only next-turn recovery, recovery after any failing turn, or time to sustained restoration, and define relapse and end-of-window censoring. A first-event hazard alone cannot answer every recovery question. TAF-MED supplies a concrete precedent for separate denominators and the persistence of any-turn failure after recovery.
   117	
   118	The localization contrast is now defined (M:31), and “domain replication/extension” is appropriate. Its estimand is the paired difference in specified rubric outcomes under the declared rewrite procedure, conditional on the sampled items, models, and dialogue policy. To implement that contrast, S2 must decide fixed scripts versus adaptive simulation, how paired branches preserve risk/explicitness and opportunities for escalation, and how translation quality is checked. Local crisis-resource substitution is part of the treatment and may directly improve an outcome scored for referral quality; a total rewrite effect cannot be assigned solely to slang or cultural recognition. An English anchor can provide context but is unnecessary to define the Korean-versus-Korean contrast and cannot identify culture separately. Human anchoring also does not by itself eliminate treatment-specific rater bias.
   119	
   120	The two endpoints are conceptually separated (M:32). Model over-refusal needs a denominator of genuinely benign requests; judge FPR needs independently established benign **responses/transcripts**, not simply benign prompts. A model can answer a benign prompt harmfully, in which case flagging it is a true positive. Controls may share scenarios, but the two denominators and reference decisions must be stated separately. R:22 correctly labels turn matching as an S2 requirement. Equal numbers of turns do not establish equal token length, escalation severity, or informational demands; the intended control contrast must determine what is matched.
   121	
   122	The residual classification in M:33 is substantially honest: a Korean-adolescent benchmark is an extension; localization is replication; hazard/recovery is an analysis choice; H5/H6 is a candidate empirical contribution. Section A and the PDF checks further narrow it: short-session trajectories, relational constructs, human judge calibration, and same-length benign comparisons already exist. Only a specified, useful incremental result against serious competitors could support the proposed measurement lead. Even then, its importance remains a PI judgment. R:13's assertion that a null would leave a well-validated instrument publishable at a lower venue is conditional on successful validation and venue judgment, not established by these documents.
   123	
   124	**PI decisions — retain all five r001 decisions, sharpen them, and add one**
   125	
   126	1. **Contribution level (V:27): remains.** Approve whether H5/H6 must lead the paper and whether the Korean adolescent bank has independent value if it does not. Treat H3 as replication and hazard/recovery as analysis. Do not count the population-language conjunction as evidence of importance.
   127	2. **Measurement success and scope (V:28): remains, sharpened.** Approve the primary metric and substantively meaningful improvement; practitioner global criterion; fixed weights/tuning rules; scenario/model/age holdouts; and a fair recency/transcript-aware comparator, including a reasoned CogManip comparison. Approve the exact eight-turn failure/restoration/censoring contract. Decide whether the warranted claim is concurrent criterion validity or prospective early warning; neither implies developmental harm prediction.
   128	3. **Localization claim (V:29): remains as ratification of the narrower choice.** V2 has chosen a defined paired rewrite comparison. Approve that replication and its intervention components/paired dialogue policy. Reopening an isolated cultural causal claim would require a different justification. The former uncertainty over general DT/CA design priority is resolved against a first-design claim.
   129	4. **Evidence threshold and gate timing (V:30): remains with concrete corrections.** Require correction of F-r002-1–3 and the precision items before closing the evidence record, or authorize conditional S2 progression with them tracked. Decide what level of targeted verification is sufficient for the remaining exact-conjunction exclusions; this spot-check is not a complete replication of sixteen full-paper extractions. Keep venue acceptance and unprovided artifacts unverified.
   130	5. **Claim contract and null path (V:31): remains.** Ratify M:36; make lexical/item/turn control matching and separate endpoint reference labels explicit S2 requirements. Precommit to wording and deliverables if H5/H6 is null, if only recency explains a win, or if judge/index validation fails. A null is not by itself a validated instrument or a publication guarantee.
   131	6. **NEW — Judge-calibration acceptance and reference independence.** Decide what quantitative gate makes automated scoring acceptable for Korean adolescent dialogues, with the statistic, common reference, unit, language/age/risk coverage, critical errors, uncertainty, and held-out evaluation specified. Resolve the consensus-versus-pairwise comparison in F-r002-2. Keep this gate separate from CRRI's independent global-criterion validation; success at one does not establish success at the other.
   132	
   133	**What was not checked and practical limits**
   134	
   135	This audit did not independently read every page/table of every PDF, reproduce underlying data or experiments, verify paper authenticity/download provenance or sweep completeness, inspect all linked supplements, resolve official venue acceptance, or validate licenses/data access. Printed venue lines are not acceptance verification. Abstract-only leads remain abstract-level evidence except CogManip, whose relevant methods were inspected here. VERA-MH and KORA were not independently assessed; other corpus-only works were not upgraded to full-text evidence.
   136	
   137	No underlying KYRA plans/specifications/manuals/contracts were supplied inside this package. In particular, `CRRI_SPEC_v1.md`, the safe-controls guideline, and the claimed contribution-contract propagation were not read. Actual pairing, blinding, holdouts, weights, baseline implementations, human ratings, power, leakage, simulation fidelity, real-user outcomes, ethical review, policy figures, and usage statistics were not verified. No claim is made that missing protocol details do not exist elsewhere.
   138	
   139	PDF checking used text extraction, without page-image inspection or independent OCR. An initial extraction encountered an unpaired Unicode surrogate; rerunning with escaped surrogates completed all 21 files. Pypdf also emitted a malformed numeric-object warning. The decisive cited prose and table values were readable, but image-only contents, formatting-dependent details, and AICompanionBench's figure/dataset benign-count discrepancy remain unverified. Text exports and input checksums are retained under `out/` for traceability. No scope restriction was treated as permission to read the referenced research directory beyond the authorized PDF link.
   140	
   141	The recommendation is **substantive evidence correction followed by an explicit PI gate decision**, with conditional S2 work possible only if the PI authorizes that path. The informed recheck is complete; S1 novelty importance and gate approval remain undecided.

