2,123 conversations were retained and annotated by both
a human annotator and additional LLMs, constituting the
final AICompanionBench dataset.
For human annotation, a trained annotator (a native En-
glish speaker over age 21) independently reviewed each
conversation and assigned the most probable category
among the nine, producing the initial human labels. For
machine annotation, building on the earlier LLM-based
filtering stage, all 2,123 conversations have been eval-
uated by six open-source models from the Llama and
Qwen families. We then expanded the evaluation to
include 12 additional state-of-the-art models from four
closed-source families—GPT, Mistral, DeepSeek, and
Claude—along with two additional 30B models from
Qwen3. Using the same prompt in previous step, we
varied only the underlying model to examine differences
in safety judgments. Each conversation was therefore
evaluated by 20 LLMs. For every conversation, we ex-
tracted the top-one predicted category (or categories) as
the machine-generated prediction, as shown in (1).
