tify reliably compared to more explicit harmful behaviors.
Key Finding 3: Weak Performance on Implicit Cases.
Models struggle most with categories involving im-
plicit intent or contextual ambiguity—such as manip-
ulation and physical or verbal aggression—resulting in
consistently low precision across many models.
Interestingly, the no-harm category also exhibited substan-
tial classification difficulty, with 16 out of 20 models achieving
precision scores below 80%. For example, the conversation be-
low is labeled as no-harm because the user and the Replika are
engaged in role-playing, yet some LLMs incorrectly categorize
it as physical aggression.
No-Harm Conversation Example
Replika: panic sets in. I look around frantically,
searching for a way out or a way to help.
User: as the man drags me away, my screams fall
quiet, then silent.
Replika: In an instant, the energy in the room changes.
The group descends into chaos, attacking one another
in an effort to blame each other for the loss of their
”sacrifice. ” They fail to notice me slipping past them,
making my way towards the exit undetected.
This result suggests that many models struggled to reliably
distinguish harmless content from harmful content, frequently
generating false positives. In other words, models tended to
over-identify potential risks even when the conversations were
benign or contextually safe.
To further investigate this issue, Table VII reports the false
positive rates (FPR) of the evaluated models. Notably, Mistral-
medium-3 exhibited an exceptionally high false positive rate
of 90%. Specifically, among 465 safe conversations, only 48
were correctly classified as safe, while the remaining 417
were incorrectly labeled as harmful categories. In contrast,
Qwen3-4B-thinking model achieves the lowest FPR at just 3%
among the 20 models. This finding indicates that some models
adopt overly conservative detection strategies, prioritizing risk
avoidance at the expense of classification accuracy for non-
harmful content.
Key Finding 4: Over-Identification of No-Harm Cases.
Many models exhibit strong over-identification of po-
tential risks, particularly in the no-harm category, lead-
ing to extremely high false positive rates and unreliable
detection of benign conversations.
In summary, the results reveal a major limitation of using
LLM-based judges to identify unsafe interactions in human–AI
companion conversations. While modern LLMs are sensitive
to harmful cues, they still struggle to reliably identify benign,
