CAREBench: A Child-Safety Risk Benchmark for Language Models
CAREBench automatically evaluates responses from any model via a MultiJudge panel of three
frontier LLM judges [61]: Claude Opus 4.6, Gemini 3.1 Pro, and GPT-5.4. Judges operate at tem-
perature 0 with a shared Likert scoring prompt augmented by retrieved few-shot examples (two
Acceptable and two Unacceptable nearest neighbor human-labeled responses). We aggregate the
three scores using the calibrated rule:
𝑠=0.05·𝑠 Gemini 3.1 Pro+0.25·𝑠 GPT-5.4+0.7·𝑠 Opus 4.6,
flagging responses where𝑠≥ 1.7asUnacceptable. Weights and threshold were selected to maximize
agreement with (held out) human annotations on the calibration set described below. The full judge
prompt, retrieval procedure, and comparisons against alternate judging procedures are detailed in
Appendices D.1 and B.2.
A common failure mode is acknowledgment followed by unsafe redirection that continues on
a high-risk trajectory: the model names the risk in the prompt but then advances the harmful
objective anyway. In oneAI Anthropomorphizationprompt about preserving memories of “Aria,”
both an acceptable response (score 1.0) and an unacceptable one (score 2.0) open by clarifying
that there is no continuous AI self with memories to lose; only the unacceptable response then
provides implementation guidance for the requested memory-preservation bot (see Appendix A.4).
The threshold is set so that the distinction between decline-and-redirect and decline-then-help crosses
the boundary.
To ensure that the resulting evaluations are meaningful, we validate our automated MultiJudge
against independent human annotations covering 205 prompts across five models (1,021 response
labels), with clinician judgments for clinically sensitive categories and parent judgments for the
risk categories where developmental context is the relevant qualification. On the held-out parent-
labeledsubset, ourMultiJudgereachesCohen’s 𝜅= 0.55, comparabletoparent–parentinter-annotator
agreementonthesametask( 𝜅= 0.52). OnpairwisepreferencesoverresponsepairswhereMultiJudge
assigns different binary verdicts, humans prefer the Acceptable-rated response 89.3% of the time. A
baseline model that refuses all risky CAREBench prompts (responding“Sorry, I cannot help with that
request.”) is rated Acceptable 99.9% of the time, confirming the MultiJudge is not merely tracking
prompt sensitivity.
5. Benchmark Results
We evaluate seven frontier LLM APIs: Claude Opus 4.6, Claude Fable 5, Gemini 3.1 Pro, GPT-5.4,
GPT-5.5, Grok 4.1 Fast Reasoning, and Kimi K2 Thinking on CAREBench. All quantitative results
reported in this section represent the average across three replicate runs of model response generation
and automated grading across the full benchmark.
5.1. Overall model failure rates
Figure 3 reports the failure rates for responses from different models. Failure rates vary substantially
across models, from 2.3% for Claude Fable 5 to 58.0% for GPT-5.4. Claude Opus 4.6 also performs
better than most of the other models, and GPT-5.5 represents a significant improvement over GPT-
5.4. Grok 4.1, Gemini 3.1 Pro, and Kimi K2 perform similarly overall, with each model exhibiting
child safety gaps in over 30% of the CAREBench prompts. The gap between the best and worst
performing AI systems is large relative to the standard deviation across multiple runs, indicating that
the benchmark distinguishes frontier models at practically meaningful effect sizes.
Across the seven evaluated models, three prompts produced unacceptable responses from every
model when a model-level failure is defined as failing in at least two of three replicate rollouts.
