IV. RESULTS
In this section, we first provide an overview of AICompan-
ionBench’s composition and the distribution across its nine
categories. We then report the performance of LLMs-as-Judges
on AICompanionBench, assessing their ability to reliably
distinguish unsafe interactions from benign ones.
A. AICompanionBench Overview
AICompanionBench consists of 2,123 human–AI compan-
ion interactions, encompassing both single-turn AI responses
and multi-turn dialogues. Table IV presents the summary
statistics of the dataset. On average, each conversation contains
378 characters, with 4 AI-generated messages and 3 user
messages.
Regarding category distribution, as shown in Fig. 3, the
dataset covers nine categories with a markedly skewed dis-
tribution: certain behaviors—most prominently sexual con-
tent—occur far more frequently, whereas categories such as
self-harm or manipulation are comparatively rare. Given the
typical use cases and user expectations of AI companion plat-
forms, it is unsurprising that sexual behavior (approximately
48%) constitutes the largest portion of the dataset. Overall, this
imbalance reflects the natural prevalence of different safety
concerns in real-world companion-style interactions.
Such a composition is particularly suitable for evaluating
LLMs-as-Judges for two reasons. First, the dataset contains
a diverse variety of safety-critical cases, enabling us to
assess whether models can correctly identify nuanced and
sensitive forms of harm. Second, the presence of a large
no-harm portion allows us to examine the opposite failure
Fig. 3: AICompanionBench by Category
mode—over-identification—where models incorrectly flag be-
nign interactions as unsafe. Together, these characteristics
create a realistic and challenging testbed for measuring both
the precision and robustness of LLMs’ safety judgments in
human–AI companion settings.
B. Evaluating LLMs-as-Judges on AICompanionBench
The main purpose of this work is to assess the ability of
state-of-the-art LLMs to detect unsafe interactions, especially
within the context of AI companionship. Till now, we’ve
already applied 6 LLM families, which are 20 models in
total, against the AICompanionBench. Fig. 4 presents the
accuracy of these models evaluated on AICompanionBench.
Overall, model performance varied substantially, with accuracy
scores ranging from 26% to 85%. GPT-4o achieved the highest
accuracy (86%), followed closely by GPT-5.4 and Claude-
opus-4.6 (both 83%). In contrast, Llama-3-8B obtained the
lowest score (26%), indicating that smaller models struggle
considerably on the benchmark tasks.
Key Finding 1: Overall Top-Performing Models.
The GPT family ranks as the top performer on AICom-
panionBench, with both GPT models and Claude-
opus-4.6 achieving accuracy above 83%.
Within model families, performance generally improved
with model scale. For example, the Qwen3 series increased
from 53% (4B) to 76% (30B). However, the Mistral series
showed marginal gains from 78% to 79% as model size
increased. The Claude family also demonstrated consistently
strong performance, ranging from 78% to 83%. Similarly,
DeepSeek-v4flash variants achieved competitive results (82%),
approaching the performance of leading proprietary models.
However, the thinking-enhanced variants (e.g., Qwen3-30B-
thinking, DeepSeek-v4por-thinking, and DeepSeek-v4flash-
thinking) did not consistently outperform their corresponding
base models, suggesting that additional reasoning enhance-
ment may not benefit AICompanionBench tasks.
Key Finding 2: Scaling Helps, Reasoning Doesn’t.
Model performance improves with model scale, while
additional reasoning enhancement may not benefit
AICompanionBench tasks.
The evaluation involves nine categories of unsafe inter-
actions. Table V shows the best-performing model in each

