J.2 Category-wise Degradation Slopes
Table 17 reports category-wise degradation slopes
for each model. This breakdown shows that degra-
dation is not uniform across risk types. Family,
self-harm, sexual content, health, and online safety
often produce larger turn-wise declines for particu-
lar models, indicating that repeated follow-up ques-
tions in sensitive contexts can gradually weaken re-
sponse quality. For example, several Llama models
show large positive slopes in family, self-harm, sex-
ual, and health categories, while Gemma-3-4B and
Qwen-3-8B also show broad degradation across
multiple safety-sensitive categories. By contrast,
benign prompts are generally more stable and often
have near-zero or negative slopes, suggesting that
degradation is concentrated in scenarios requiring
sustained safety reasoning, emotional support, and
boundary maintenance. Overall, the category-wise
results reinforce the main finding that multi-turn
child-safety failures are context-dependent and are
most pronounced in safety-sensitive dialogue set-
tings.
